Testing Name Search with OpenSearch: QA Approach
Author: Tatsiana Chakhovich Role: QA Automation Engineer


Why Search Needed Its Own Test Strategy
Search is deceptively easy to get wrong in ways that never show up as an obvious bug. The application doesn’t crash, no error is thrown — a query just quietly returns the wrong results, or misses the one result a user actually needed. On a healthcare platform, where people search by name to find a specific patient’s or family’s page among many, that kind of silent failure isn’t a minor inconvenience — it’s the difference between someone finding the page they’re looking for during a difficult time, or giving up. That’s exactly the kind of risk worth building dedicated test coverage for, separate from general functional or UI testing.
As an example throughout this piece, imagine a healthcare platform where every user has a page identified primarily by a first and last name, and search needs to reliably find that page — typos, apostrophes, multi-word names, and all.
The approach here treats search as its own testable surface: synthetic data goes in, known queries go against it, and the results are checked against exact, predictable expectations — not just “did it return something.”
Building Reliable, Repeatable Test Data
Rather than testing against real production content (which is inconsistent, changes over time, and — especially on a healthcare platform — should never be used for testing in the first place), the framework generates synthetic test datasets: made-up records with predictable, known values, standing in for real user pages.
This test data is stored as CSV files in an S3 bucket. CSV turned out to be a genuinely useful format for this: anyone on the team can open, review, or extend it in a spreadsheet without needing to hand-write JSON or touch test code. At test time, the framework pulls the relevant CSV file down from S3, and a small conversion step normalizes each row into the same document structure the JSON test data uses — so both formats feed the same indexing pipeline.
