Each synthetic record is indexed with a deliberately unique, traceable identifier like test-0001 above, which matters for two reasons: it lets a test clean up exactly what it created (nothing else), and it lets a test search for and verify an exact expected match instead of just checking "results came back."
Structuring Search Test Cases by Query Complexity
Search bugs tend to surface differently depending on how many terms a query has, so test cases are organized by term count. Using the example data above:
•	Single-term — searching "Jane" or "Doe" should return test-0001.
•	Two-term — searching "John O'Brien" should return test-0002, and searching "John OBrien" (no apostrophe) should return the same result.
•	Three-term / multi-term — a longer query like "Michael James Smith" needs to correctly interpret which part is a compound first name versus a compound last name.
Each category has its own set of test cases, stored as structured CSV/JSON data files rather than hardcoded in the test code — which means adding a new scenario is a data change, not a code change.
Test Scenarios That Actually Matter for Name Search
Search-by-name looks simple from the outside, but real names break naive implementations in predictable ways — which means the test scenarios worth covering are the ones that mirror how people actually type. Continuing the example data above:
•	Exact matches — searching "Jane Doe" returns test-0001.
•	Fuzzy matching for typos — insertions, deletions, transpositions, and substitutions should still find the right result: searching "Micheal Smith" or "Smth" should still surface test-0003.
•	Apostrophe normalization — searching "OBrien" or "O'Brien" should both return test-0002.
•	Multi-word names — a query like "Robert Van Nguyen" needs to correctly handle whether the middle term belongs to the first or last name.
•	Exclusion rules — no query should ever surface test-0004 (marked removed) or test-0005 (marked not publicly visible), even for an exact name match.
•	Tie-breaking behavior — when multiple results score equivalently, ordering should fall back to a consistent, predictable rule (such as recency) rather than being arbitrary.
Testing at this level — validating behavior against realistic name patterns — is deliberately kept separate from the underlying relevance-scoring implementation. The test suite doesn’t need to know or assert on the specific weighting logic a search engine uses internally; it just needs to confirm that, given a realistic query, the expected result comes back and the excluded content stays excluded. That separation is what makes the tests resilient — the ranking algorithm can be tuned and improved over time without breaking the test suite, as long as the observable behavior it’s supposed to guarantee still holds.
Additional Test Cases, by Query Type
Beyond the core scenarios above, a few more specific edge cases are worth deliberately writing test cases for — the kind that only surface once you think carefully about how real people type real names.
