Single-term queries
•	Fuzzy matching follows the search engine’s standard edit-distance rule: a 1–2 character term requires an exact match, a 3–5 character term allows one edit (an insertion, deletion, transposition, or substitution), and a longer term allows two edits — with the first character always expected to match exactly. This is standard built-in fuzzy-matching behavior, not custom logic, but it’s still worth a test case per edit type using the example data from earlier — confirming "Michaeel" (an inserted letter) still finds test-0003 ("Michael"), "Smth" (a dropped letter) still finds it by last name ("Smith"), "Micahel" (two letters swapped) still matches, and "Smyth" (a substituted letter) still matches too.
•	A single-term search should not silently expand into nickname synonyms — searching "Jack" shouldn't surface test-0002 just because "Jack" is a common nickname for "John." This is a deliberate product decision worth its own regression test, since a well-meaning "helpful" synonym feature could quietly get added later and change behavior unexpectedly.
•	Last-name matches and first-name matches aren’t treated as equally likely — a single-term search should prioritize results where the term matches on the field it’s more distinctive for. A test case here doesn’t need to assert on how much weight is applied, just that the ordering reflects that priority.
Two-term queries
•	Short second terms are a deliberate edge case: searching "John O" (a full first name plus a single initial, aimed at test-0002) should only return exact matches on both parts — no fuzzy matching kicks in for a term that short, since there's not enough information to safely guess at intent.
•	A two-term search should still find a reasonable match even when only one of the two terms matches exactly and the other is a near-miss — the test case here isn’t about the specific scoring tier, just confirming that a partial-but-close match doesn’t come back empty when a full exact match doesn’t exist.
Three-term and longer queries
•	Three-term names have two valid interpretations, and both need their own test case. Take a hypothetical name like “Sara Beth Whitfield”: one interpretation treats "Sara Beth" as the first name and "Whitfield" as the last name, while the other treats "Sara" as the first name and "Beth Whitfield" as the last name. A robust test suite checks that both interpretations can succeed, since the system can't know in advance which one is correct for a given name.
•	For four or more terms, the practical test case is confirming the system still finds a match by reasonably narrowing down which terms it treats as the “important” ones (typically the first and last), rather than trying to exact-match an entire long string, which real names with middle names, suffixes, or multiple surnames would otherwise fail.
Across all query types
•	Certain filler words (like the platform’s own name, or connector words such as “or”) get stripped from a query before matching — but a test case should confirm that a query consisting only of a filler word isn’t stripped down to nothing, since that would silently turn a real (if unusual) search into an empty one.
The Test Framework: Setup, Assertion, Teardown
Each test run follows the same reliable pattern:
1.	Setup — load the synthetic test data from the S3-stored CSV/JSON files, check whether matching test documents already exist in the index (leftover from a previous run), and delete them if so.
2.	Index — bulk-index the fresh synthetic documents into the search index using their unique IDs.
3.	Query — call the search API with a specific term (or terms) that should match one of the known synthetic records.
4.	Assert — verify not just that results came back, but that an exact match for the expected record is present among them (and that excluded records, like the removed or non-visible rows, never appear).
5.	Teardown — clean up the synthetic test documents so the index isn’t left polluted for the next run or for anyone else querying it.
This setup/assert/teardown discipline is what makes the tests trustworthy to run repeatedly and in any order — a test never depends on leftover state from a previous run, and never leaves anything behind for the next one.
Design Trade-offs Worth Knowing
A few decisions in this approach are deliberate, not incidental:
•	Behavior tests, not implementation tests. As covered above, the suite asserts on outcomes (the right result comes back, the wrong one is excluded) rather than on internal scoring details. This costs a little precision — a test can’t tell you why a result ranked where it did — but it buys resilience: the underlying ranking logic can be retuned or even swapped for a different engine entirely without rewriting the test suite.
•	Synthetic data over sampled production data. It would be faster to grab a few real records and anonymize them, but anonymization is easy to get subtly wrong, and on a healthcare platform that risk isn’t worth the time saved. Fully synthetic data with obviously fake names removes the question entirely.
•	CSV as the primary authoring format. JSON is more expressive, but the team members who most often need to add a new search test case aren’t necessarily the ones most comfortable writing JSON by hand. Optimizing for who actually edits the test data, not just for technical elegance, kept the test suite easier to maintain over time.
What This Approach Catches
This kind of testing is aimed squarely at regressions that are invisible anywhere else in the stack:
•	A change to indexing logic that silently drops a field search relies on
•	A relevance/ranking change that pushes an exact match out of the visible results
•	Indexing delays or failures that mean newly created content doesn’t become searchable when it should
•	Filtering logic (like permission or visibility rules) not being applied correctly to search results
None of these would necessarily fail a unit test or show up in manual UI testing — they only surface when you actually index real-shaped data and query it the way a user would.
Lessons Learned
•	Never test search against production data. Synthetic, disposable test data with known expected outcomes is the only way to make assertions precise instead of approximate — especially critical on a healthcare platform, where production data is real people’s information.
•	Give every test document a traceable ID. It’s what makes cleanup safe and exact-match assertions possible.
•	Keep test cases as data, not code. Storing search scenarios as structured CSV/JSON files rather than hardcoded strings makes the test suite easy for anyone to extend, not just whoever wrote the original framework.
•	Always clean up after yourself. A search test suite that leaves synthetic data behind will eventually pollute real search results or cause the next test run to behave unpredictably.
